# The changelog and the release notes are append-only lists, and the
# standard gives an entry one place to go, so two branches each writing
# one arrive at the same anchor: a conflict on the insertion point, with
# nothing to decide. `union` resolves it by keeping both sides' added
# lines, ours first and then theirs, the base's own lines staying where
# they are, and it is built into git — nobody has to configure anything
# for it to apply.
#
# Which side is `ours` is decided by the operation and not by whose work
# it is: merging, `ours` is the branch checked out and `theirs` the
# branch being merged into it; rebasing, `ours` is the upstream and
# `theirs` each commit being replayed onto it.
#
# The rebase is where the driver earns its keep, and it has a price
# worth knowing: on a checkout these two files never conflict at all, so
# the same existing entry edited on two branches merges in silence
# rather than being flagged. That is why neither states how many entries
# it has — a count is exactly such an edit, and union would have kept
# both numbers.
#
# The driver is a checkout's, and the forge does not apply it: a pull
# request whose changelog or release notes overlap its base is reported
# CONFLICTING however cleanly the same pair merges under the driver, and
# a rebase on a checkout is what clears it. Section 9 of the organization
# standard, btclib-org/.github's README.md, is where this rule is
# recorded.
#
# Both lines are in every repository's copy, a tree carrying no
# RELEASE_NOTES.md included: section 2 of that README.md gives the
# release notes to a repository that publishes, and an attribute on a
# path the tree does not hold matches nothing.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
RELEASE_NOTES.md merge=union

## This repository in particular

# The launchers are what this tree ships, to macOS, Windows and Linux.
# `.bat` is checked out CRLF because cmd.exe reads a batch file line by
# line and a lone LF has cost that reader a label before now, which is
# why `.pre-commit-config.yaml` keeps the line-ending fixer off those
# files and `REVIEWING.md` reads them rather than the diff. `.sh` and
# `.command` are checked out LF because a carriage return is an ordinary
# character where a line ending is expected: `#!/bin/bash` followed by
# one names an interpreter that does not exist, and a `fi` followed by
# one is not `fi`. Neither failure is macOS's in particular, so the
# rule serves every file `git ls-files '*.sh' '*.command'` names rather
# than `macos/`'s alone. `.ps1` is checked out LF too: PowerShell reads
# either, where cmd.exe does not, so there is no failure this tree
# exists to avoid on that side of a mismatch. The `binary` lines name
# what a launcher fetches; nothing tracked matches them today, and the
# day one is committed by mistake git will not try to diff or normalize
# it.
*.exe binary
*.app binary
Bitcoin-Qt binary
run_electrum binary
*.md text
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.conf text
*.json text

# */checksums.sha256 is the file the updaters write to: macOS's and Linux's
# both reach it through shared/utilities/lib.sh, whose appender writes an LF
# line, and the Windows half appends whatever line ending its writer
# defaults to. Without an eol here git stores whichever one last
# touched the file rather than converging them, so eol=lf normalizes every
# write to the format that shared appender and shasum already produce.
# keys/*.fingerprints is never written by a script, but is the same kind of
# plain text list and gets the same treatment for the same reason -- a
# second unattributed text format in the tree is one more thing to notice
# missing.
*/checksums.sha256 text eol=lf
keys/*.fingerprints text eol=lf

# There is no pyproject.toml here, so each tool's configuration is a file
# of its own and these are the formats those files are written in.
# `text` is what stores them LF in the index whatever an editor saved;
# `mixed-line-ending --fix=lf` in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` arrives at
# the same bytes, but only in a clone that has run the hook, where an
# attribute is the repository's own answer to what its text is.
# `.gitattributes` and `.gitignore` are git's configuration rather than a
# tool's and get the line for the same reason. `text` alone on each: the
# index is the invariant they want, and the `eol` below is earned rather
# than assumed.
*.yml text
*.yaml text
*.toml text
*.jsonc text
.gitattributes text
.gitignore text
.secrets.baseline text

# The remaining plain text files carry no extension, so each is named on
# its own line, git offering no alternation between them. `VERSION` earns
# the `eol`: `RELEASING.md` cuts a release tag with
# `git tag -s "v$(cat VERSION)"`, command substitution strips a trailing
# newline and not the carriage return in front of it, and
# `git check-ref-format` refuses the ref name that results. Nothing reads
# `COPYRIGHT` or `LICENSE`, so `text` is the whole of what they need.
COPYRIGHT text
LICENSE text
VERSION text eol=lf
